##### Introduction


Consider the scalar Allen–Cahn equation $$\label{eq:allen-cahn}
 \Delta u=u^3-u.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $N\ge2$, and suppose a bounded solution $u:\mathbb R^{N-1}\times\mathbb R\to(-1,1)$ of $\Delta u=W'(u)$ is strictly increasing in the last coordinate. Suppose its vertical limits $a_\pm(x')$, extended to $\mathbb R^N$, are global minimizers. Then $u$ is a global minimizer. Moreover there is a dimensional constant $C_N$ such that $$\mathcal E(u;B_R(y))\le C_N R^{N-1}
 \qquad (y\in\mathbb R^N,\ R\ge2).
 \label{eq:monotone-energy-growth}$$

There is a constant $C=C(n)$, and for each $\gamma>0$ a constant $C_\gamma=C(n,\gamma)$, such that every solution with $D>0$ satisfies $$\label{eq:diffuse-local}
 |T(x)|^2\le CD(x),\qquad |\nabla D(x)|\le CD(x),\qquad
 D(y)\le C_\gamma e^{(2\sqrt2+\gamma)|y-z|}D(z)
 \quad(y,z\in\mathbb R^n).$$ For every $R<\infty$ and integer $k\ge0$ there is $C_{R,k}=C(n,R,k)$ such that, for every center $z$, $$\label{eq:diffuse-normalized-bounds}
 \left\|\frac{D}{D(z)}\right\|_{C^k(B_R(z))}
 +\left\|\frac{T}{\sqrt{D(z)}}\right\|_{C^k(B_R(z))}
 \le C_{R,k}.$$ Moreover $\inf_{B_R(z)}D\ge C_R^{-1}D(z)$. The dependence on a growing observation radius can be made explicit: for every $k\ge0$ and $\gamma>0$, with $\kappa=2\sqrt2+\gamma$, $$\label{eq:diffuse-derivative-growth}
 \begin{split}
 |\nabla^kD(x)|&\le C_{k,\gamma}e^{\kappa|x-z|}D(z),\\
 |\nabla^kT(x)|&\le C_{k,\gamma}e^{\kappa|x-z|/2}\sqrt{D(z)}
 \qquad(x,z\in\mathbb R^n).
 \end{split}$$

A solution with $|v|<1$ has a nonempty zero set $\Sigma=\{v=0\}$. For every $0<\gamma<\sqrt2$ there is $C_\gamma=C(n,\gamma)$ such that $$\label{eq:phase-decay}
 q(x)\le C_\gamma
       e^{-(\sqrt2-\gamma)\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,\Sigma)}.$$ More generally the same estimate, with $\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,\partial\Omega)$ in place of $\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(x,\Sigma)$, holds for any solution in an open set $\Omega$ with $0<v\le1$ there, and also with the signs reversed.

Let $v_j$ be entire solutions in the fixed dimension, and let $z_j$ be zeros with $\epsilon_j=D_j(z_j)>0$ and $\epsilon_j\to0$. Translate and rotate so that $z_j=0$ and $\nu_j(0)=e_n$, and write $x=(x',s)\in\mathbb R^{n-1}\times\mathbb R$. After a subsequence, $$\label{eq:diffuse-limits}
 U_j\longrightarrow s,\qquad
 \frac{D_j}{\epsilon_j}\longrightarrow\mathcal D>0,\qquad
 \frac{T_j}{\sqrt{\epsilon_j}}\longrightarrow\mathcal T
 \quad\hbox{in }C^k_{\rm loc}\text{ for every }k\ge0.$$ Here $\mathcal D(0)=1$. The tensor $\mathcal T$ has only tangential components, is independent of $s$, is trace free, and is harmonic in $x'$. The positive function $F(x',s)=G(s)\mathcal D(x',s)$ obeys $$\label{eq:diffuse-limit-operator}
 \mathcal L_0 F=2G(s)|\mathcal T(x')|^2,
 \qquad \mathcal L_0=-\Delta+2-G(s).$$ Moreover $$\label{eq:diffuse-mean-limit}
 \frac{H_j(0)}{\epsilon_j}\longrightarrow\frac12F_s(0).$$

Let $F\ge0$ be a smooth entire function on $\mathbb R^{n-1}\times\mathbb R$ and suppose $\mathcal L_0 F=S$, where $S\ge0$ is smooth and $S(x',s)=S(x',-s)$. There is a decomposition $$\label{eq:diffuse-Riesz}
 F=E+h,\qquad E\ge0,\quad h\ge0,\quad
 \mathcal L_0 E=S,\quad \mathcal L_0 h=0,
 \quad E(x',s)=E(x',-s).$$ In particular $E_s(x',0)=0$ and $\nabla' E_s(x',0)=0$. With integrals permitted to have value $+\infty$, $$\label{eq:diffuse-sharp-positive}
 \frac{|F_s(0)|}{2}
 \le\frac{1}{2\sqrt2}\,\frac1\sigma
                       \int_\mathbb RG(s)^2F(0,s)\,\,\mathrm ds.$$ For every $c>1/(2\sqrt2)$ there exists $P=P(c)<\infty$ such that $$\label{eq:diffuse-positive-window}
 \frac{|F_s(0)|}{2}
 \le\frac{c}{\sigma}\int_{-P}^P G(s)^2F(0,s)\,\,\mathrm ds$$ for all such $F$. The choice of $P$ is independent of the source and of $F$.*

*More precisely, every extreme point of $$\mathcal K=\{h\ge0:\mathcal L_0 h=0\text{ on }\mathbb R^n,\ h(0)=1\}$$ has the form $e^{b\cdot x'}f(s/\sqrt2)$, where $a=\sqrt{4-2|b|^2}\in[1,2]$. If $a>1$, $f$ is one of $$\label{eq:diffuse-extremals}
 \frac1a e^{ay}(a-\tanh y),\qquad
 \frac1a e^{-ay}(a+\tanh y);$$ if $a=1$, $f(y)=\mathop{\mathrm{sech}}y$.

For every $c>1/(2\sqrt2)$ there exist $P=P(c)<\infty$ and $\delta=\delta(n,c)\in(0,1/2]$ such that every entire solution and every zero $z$ with $0<D(z)<\delta$ satisfy $$\label{eq:diffuse-mean-window}
 |H(z)|\le\frac c\sigma
     \int_{-P}^P q(z+s\nu(z))^3D(z+s\nu(z))\,\,\mathrm ds.$$ The point is regular and the normal is oriented by $p$. The inequality also holds with any larger normal interval. In particular one can fix $$\label{eq:diffuse-strict-mean}
 \frac{1}{2\sqrt2}<c_*<\frac{\sqrt2}{3}$$ and obtain constants $P_*,\delta_*>0$ used in the finite-density argument.

For any prescribed finite $P$, increasing $S$ makes $$\Phi(z,t)=z+t\nu(z),\qquad z\in\Sigma_{S/2},\quad |t|\le P,$$ injective, with Jacobian $1+\varepsilon_S$ and $q(\Phi(z,t))=G(t)+o_S(1)$, uniformly. Good-surface area in every ball of radius $r\ge1$ is at most $Cr^m$, and $$\label{eq:B1}
 \int_{\Sigma_{S/2}\cap B_r(x)}D\,\,\mathrm dS
 \le C\int_{B_{r+1}(x)}q^3D\,\,\mathrm dx
 \le Cr^{m-2}\qquad(x\in\mathbb R^n,\ r\ge1).$$ For $y\in Z$, put $r=|z-y|$ on the surface and $e_r=(z-y)/r$. Uniformly in $y$, $$\label{eq:B2}
 \int_{\Sigma_S\cap\{r>R_0\}}
 \left((e_r\cdot\nu)^2r^{-m}+|H|r^{1-m}\right)\,\mathrm dS
 \le \eta(R_0),\qquad \eta(R_0)\longrightarrow0.$$ The same function $\eta$ may be used for every $S$ above one fixed unit-tube threshold $S_*$. Choose a smooth ambient $0\le\chi\le1$, supported in $\{s>S\}$ and equal to one on $\{s\ge2S\}$, with bounded first two derivatives. For fixed nonnegative Lipschitz $F$, supported in $[1/2,2]$, and every $\delta>0$, $S$ can be chosen so that $$\label{eq:B3}
 \liminf_{R\to\infty}R^{-m}
 \int_{\Sigma}\chi F(r/R)\,\,\mathrm dS
 \ge K m\omega_m\int_0^\infty t^{m-1}F(t)\,\,\mathrm dt-\delta.$$ Finally, for each fixed $R_0\ge10$, $$\label{eq:B4}
 \limsup_{R\to\infty}\frac{1}{\sigma\log R}
 \int_{\{R_0/2<|x-y|<4R\}}q^3D\,|x-y|^{2-m}\,\,\mathrm dx
 \le K m\omega_m\frac{(m-2)^2}{4}.$$ The limiting estimates are uniform for $y\in Z$.

Suppose that <a href="#eq:B6" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:B6">[eq:B6]</a> holds, with $L\le2$, and that $\psi(l)$ is bounded uniformly on each fixed range $S<s<A$. For fixed sufficiently large $S$ and $R_0\ge10$, all caps $T_0>0$, all $y\in Z$, and large $R$, one has $$\label{eq:B7}
 \begin{split}
 R^{-m}\int_\Sigma w\chi F(r/R)\,\,\mathrm dS
 &\le L\int_{\Sigma_S\cap\{R_0<r<2R\}}
       r^{2-m}\bigl(|H(z)|+|H(\pi(z))|\bigr)\,\,\mathrm dS\\
 &\quad+C_{R_0,S}+o_{R\to\infty;R_0,S}(\log R)
       +T_0\eta(R_0).
 \end{split}$$ Here $C_{R_0,S}$ is independent of $T_0,y,R$. The $o(\log R)$ remainder is independent of $T_0$ and uniform in $y$; $\eta(R_0)\to0$ is uniform in $y$. These constants may depend on the fixed function $\psi$.

Let $g(t)=\tanh(t/\sqrt2)$ and $V_g(t)=3g(t)^2-1$. For each fixed $a_0\in\mathbb R$, the function $$\label{eq:reach-half-line-barrier}
 v_{a_0}(t)=g'(t)\left[1+\int_{a_0}^t(g'(s))^{-2}
                         \int_s^\infty g'(r)\,\,\mathrm dr\,\,\mathrm ds\right],
 \qquad t\ge a_0,$$ satisfies $$(-\partial_t^2+V_g)v_{a_0}=1,\qquad
 0<c_{a_0}\le v_{a_0}\le C_{a_0}.$$ Every fixed derivative of $v_{a_0}$ is bounded on this half-line. The constants can be chosen uniformly when $a_0$ varies over a fixed compact interval. Translating the independent variable gives the same assertion for a translated profile and half-line.

There are uniform $c,c',C,\gamma>0$ such that, for sufficiently large $d(z)$ and $h$ in <a href="#eq:C1" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:C1">[eq:C1]</a>, the zero set in $B_{ch}(z)$ is a single graph over $T_z\Sigma$, extending over the tangent disk of radius $c'h$. On a smaller concentric portion, $$\label{eq:C2}
 \begin{gathered}
 |A|+h^{1/2}[A]_{C^{1/2}}\le C h^{-1},\qquad
 |\nabla_\Sigma A|\le C h^{-2},\qquad |H|\le C h^{-2},\\
 |\nabla_\Sigma H|+|\nabla_\Sigma^2H|\le C h^{-3-\gamma}.
 \end{gathered}$$ The estimates hold uniformly on every inner patch having a fixed fractional margin from the original chart boundary. Hölder norms may be computed in its graph coordinates.

There are uniform $r_0,C,\gamma>0$ with the following property. If an open ball of radius $r\ge r_0$ is disjoint from $\Sigma$ and its boundary contains $x\in\Sigma$, and $N$ is its inward normal at $x$, then $$\label{eq:C5}
 x\in\Sigma_{\rm reg},\qquad D(x)\le C/r,
 \qquad a(x)\le C/r,\qquad H_N(x)\le Cr^{-1-\gamma}.$$ After changing the sign of $v$ so that it is positive inside the ball, along the inward radius one also has $$\label{eq:C6}
 U(x+sN)\ge s-C(1+s)/r\qquad(0\le s\le\log r).$$ Only the last curvature estimate is one-sided; no absolute-value improvement for $H_N$ is asserted.

Given $\eta_0>0$, the penalty parameters can be chosen, in the order specified above, so that the following holds for all sufficiently large $d$. The function $d$ is locally semiconcave on $\Sigma_{\rm reg}$, and the singular part of its distributional surface Laplacian is nonpositive. Almost everywhere, $$\label{eq:C7}
 |\nabla_\Sigma d|\le
 C_\Gamma\exp\{C_\Gamma\sqrt{\log(K(d)+1)}\}.$$ At an active non-plateau or diagonal contact, $$\label{eq:C8}
 \Delta_\Sigma d\le(1+\eta_0+C_\Gamma d^{-\gamma})a^2d
                  +B\,\frac{|\nabla_\Sigma d|^2}{d},
 \qquad B\le1+C_\Gamma d^{-\gamma}.$$ If $ad$ is sufficiently small, the same bound can be used with $B\le\eta_0+C_\Gamma ad+C_\Gamma d^{-\gamma}$, and then $|\nabla_\Sigma d|\ge c_\Gamma>0$. At an active plateau contact, meaning $s\ge1-\delta_2$, one has $$\label{eq:C9}
 \Delta_\Sigma d\le
 G_1\bigl(|H(z)|+H_{N_y}(y)_+\bigr)
 +\eta_0\frac{|\nabla_\Sigma d|^2}{d},\qquad N_y=-e.$$ For any measurable choice of such contacts, the positive foot charge can be transferred to $y$ with multiplicity at most one and area factor $$\label{eq:C10}
 J_{y\to z}=
 \frac{|\det(\mathrm{Id}+\ell A_{N_y}(y))|}{s}\le C_\Gamma,
 \qquad d(y)\le d(z)=G_1\ell,
 \qquad a(y)+D(y)\le C_\Gamma/d(z).$$ More precisely, $J_{y\to z}\le1+C\delta_2+C a(y)\ell$ whenever $a(y)\ell$ is bounded by a sufficiently small fixed constant. The constants and the large-reach threshold are fixed before any later defect threshold, collar scale, or exhaustion radius is chosen.

For every $\varepsilon>0$ there are $S\ge10$ and $\delta>0$ such that, if $z\in\Sigma$ and $D(z)<\delta$, all zeros in $B_{4S}(z)$ belong to one regular graph converging smoothly to the tangent plane as the threshold decreases, and $$\label{eq:L2}
 |\nabla_\Sigma H(z)|^2\le
 \frac{\varepsilon}{S^m}\int_{B_S(z)\cap\Sigma_{\rm reg}}D^2\,\,\mathrm dS.$$

Let $\Omega\subset\Sigma_{\rm reg}$ be open and $\Phi(z,s)=z+s\nu(z)$. Fix $0<\alpha<1/4$ and an even smooth function $\beta(z,s)=\beta_0(sa(z))$, equal to $1$ for $|s|a\le\alpha/2$ and supported in $|s|a<\alpha$. For a compactly supported Lipschitz amplitude on the lifted tube, write $\xi_j$ for its local inverse branches and put $\xi=(\sum_j\xi_j^2)^{1/2}$. Then $\xi$ is an admissible physical test. Its quadratic form is the lifted form minus $\int\mathcal V\,\,\mathrm dx$, where the nonnegative variance density is $$\mathcal V=\min_{b\in\mathbb R^n}\sum_j|\nabla\xi_j-\xi_j b|^2.$$ For the lifted amplitude $G(s)\beta(z,s)\psi(z)$, the lifted form is $$\label{eq:L4}
 \int_{\Omega\times\mathbb R}\left\{
 G^2J|\nabla(\beta\psi)|^2+
 \beta^2\psi^2\left[-J_sGG'
       +3\bigl(v(\Phi)^2-g(s)^2\bigr)G^2J\right]\right\}
       \,\,\mathrm dS\,\,\mathrm ds,
 \qquad J=\det(\mathrm{Id}+sA).$$ Here the gradient and volume use the pullback Euclidean metric. Formula <a href="#eq:L4" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:L4">[eq:L4]</a> also holds when $\psi$ includes a bounded Lipschitz factor depending on $x=\Phi(z,s)$, initially with compact support and a finite normal cap.

For each $\varepsilon>0$, choose $P$ sufficiently large as in Lemma <a href="#lem:interface-sharp-means" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:interface-sharp-means">28</a>. There exists $\delta>0$ such that every compactly supported Lipschitz function on $\{z\in\Sigma: D(z)<\delta\}$ satisfies $$\label{eq:L3}
 (1-\varepsilon)\int_\Sigma\frac{a^2+I_P}{2}\psi^2\,\,\mathrm dS
 \le\int_\Sigma|\nabla_\Sigma\psi|^2\,\,\mathrm dS.$$ In the lifted computation the gradient coefficient can be made arbitrarily close to $\sigma$, and the negative coefficient arbitrarily close to $\sigma(a^2+I_P)/2$, with the same choices.

Let $\mathcal X$ be a maximal unit-separated subset of $X$, with $\mathcal X=\varnothing$ when $X=\varnothing$. For an arbitrary center $x_c$, set $\chi_R(x)=\exp(-\sqrt{1+|x-x_c|^2}/R)$. There are $C_D,R_D<\infty$, independent of $0<\delta\le1/4$ and $x_c$, such that for $R\ge R_D$, $$\label{eq:L11}
 \int_{\Sigma\setminus X}(D^2+E^2)\chi_R^2\,\,\mathrm dS
 \le C_D\left\{
 \int_{\Sigma\setminus X}(a^4+DE)\chi_R^2\,\,\mathrm dS
             +\sum_{x\in\mathcal X}\chi_R(x)^2\right\}.$$

Under the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#lem:interface-log-windows" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:interface-log-windows">36</a>, for each $\varepsilon>0$ there is $P_\varepsilon<\infty$ such that every fixed $P\ge P_\varepsilon$ has the following properties when $N$ is sufficiently large. If a different sheet is within $C_0\log N$ of the base, then $$\label{eq:L12}
 I_P(z)\ge(32-\varepsilon)e^{-\sqrt2\ell_{\min}(z)}.$$ If $x$ lies between two adjacent sheets, their distances $s_i,s_j$ from $x$ are both at most $C_0\log N$, they are the nearest sheets, and $|s_i-s_j|\le C_1$ for a fixed $C_1$, then $$\label{eq:L13}
 q(x)\le(4+\varepsilon)
       \left(e^{-\sqrt2s_i}+e^{-\sqrt2s_j}\right).$$ Largeness of $N$ may depend on all the fixed parameters, including $P,C_1,\varepsilon$. The window geometry and conclusions are uniform in the solution and center.

Fix $0<\tau\le .001$ among the interface tolerances, before the final choice of $\delta$, and choose the preceding data with this tolerance. There exist $L_0,t_*,c_0>0$ and $R_1,C<\infty$ with the following property. Let $f:[t_{\min},\infty)\to(0,1]$ be nonincreasing and locally Lipschitz, equal to one on $[t_{\min},t_*]$, and a positive multiple of $e^{-\kappa t/2}$ for sufficiently large $t$. Suppose also that $|f'|\le C_f^{(0)}f$ for some finite constant. Then for every center and $R\ge R_1$, writing $s_X=\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(\cdot,X)$, $$\label{eq:M3}
 \begin{aligned}
 &(1-\tau)\int_{\Sigma_g\cap\{s_X\ge4L_0\}}S_0 f(d)^2\chi^2+c_0N_R\\
 &\qquad\le(1+\tau)\int_{\Sigma_g}\chi^2 f'(d)^2|\nabla_\Sigma d|^2
       +\frac C{R^2}\int_{\Sigma_g}\chi^2f(d)^2.
 \end{aligned}$$ In particular $c_0$ is independent of $f$. The constants $L_0,t_*,R_1,C$ can also be chosen independently of $f$; its stated tail and slope conditions are used only to justify exhaustion.

Write $s_j=2^jR$, $j\ge0$; a shell $\rho\asymp s_j$ may be enlarged by any fixed factor. The good surface area and ambient volume within $C s_j^{\beta_0}$ of $Z$ on this shell are bounded by $C K(s_j)s_j^{m-4+2\alpha+n\beta_0}$. Each of the following two shell bounds has negligible sum: $$\label{eq:R3}
 C K(s)s^{-2+2\alpha+n\beta_0}(1+\log s)^C
       (L+\log s)^{1-\vartheta},$$ $$\label{eq:R4}
 C K(s)e^{C\sqrt{\log(K(s)+1)}}s^{-2}
       (L+\log s)^{3-\vartheta}.$$ Moreover, for some $\epsilon'>0$ and every fixed $R,L$, $$\label{eq:R5}
 \int_{\{\rho\asymp s\}\cap(\Sigma\setminus X)}W_*\,\mathrm dS
 \le C_Rs^{m-\epsilon'}\qquad(s\longrightarrow\infty).$$

With the fixed parameter choices above, for large $R$ and $B$, $$\label{eq:R6}
\begin{split}
 &\int_\Sigma\phi m_c^2W_*
 \left[
 \frac{3.44+24(\mathcal F/W_*)T_r}{\rho^2}
 +\frac{.012}{d}|\nabla d|^2
 +\frac{\lambda}{b'}|\nabla(c\tau-b(d))|^2
 \right]\,\mathrm dS\\
 &\hspace{2em}\le .73\mathcal L+o_R(\mathcal A)+o_{B\to\infty;R}(1).
\end{split}$$

With the preceding fixed parameter choices, for sufficiently large $R$ and uniformly in the subsequent outer radius $B$, $$\label{eq:R7}
 .895\mathcal L\le1.005\int_\Sigma|\nabla_\Sigma\psi|^2\,\mathrm dS
                  +o_R(\mathcal A)+o_{B\to\infty;R}(1).$$ The constructed ambient test has normalized switching cost at most $.087853\mathcal L$, apart from negligible collar and cutoff terms.
